# Line endings are LF everywhere, in the index and in the working tree.
#
# Every tracked text file is already LF in the index, and Prettier's
# `endOfLine` default is "lf". Without this rule `core.autocrlf=true` checks
# them out as CRLF on Windows, so `pnpm format` rewrote all ~89 otherwise
# untouched files on every run and `pnpm format:check` failed on a fresh
# clone. Pinning eol=lf makes git and Prettier agree on both platforms.
#
# This changes the working tree only. The index is LF already, so no
# renormalisation commit is needed and no file content changes.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Large binary CAD exports go to LFS.
#
# Only *.stl was listed here, so the STEP and glTF exports of the same two
# mechanisms went in as ordinary blobs — about 135 MB of them, which is most of
# why .git is 1.3 GB for a site whose source is 44 KLOC.
#
# Adding the patterns fixes this going forward only. Git does not retroactively
# move a blob already in history, so the existing .stp and .gltf objects stay
# put until someone runs:
#
#   git lfs migrate import --include="*.stp,*.gltf" --everything
#
# That rewrites every commit that touched them and needs a force-push plus a
# re-clone by anyone else working on the repo, so it is a deliberate decision
# rather than a cleanup. Nothing breaks if it never happens; the repo just
# stays large.
*.stl filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.stp filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.gltf filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# Browser-loaded previews must be ordinary Git blobs. Hosting providers that
# do not hydrate LFS pointers otherwise serve the pointer text at the model
# URL, which makes GLTFLoader try (and fail) to parse `version https://...` as
# JSON. These two previews are small enough to keep in the repository.
*.glb -filter -diff -merge binary
